ReelEdit 0.3 beta 3
===================

ReelEdit is a video editor for RISC OS, on the MLT multimedia framework
(the engine of Shotcut and Kdenlive), with the Raspberry Pi's video
hardware through riscos-reelhwaccel: a Viewer and a Timeline with a video
track (V1), an overlay track (V2: logos, picture in picture), a titles
track over it (a title can go over a logo or a picture in picture) and
audio tracks; cutting, trimming, rolls, copy and paste, fades, dissolves,
marks, speed, wipes, a clip's colour and looks, pan and zoom, freeze
frames, colour clips and countdowns, special effects, keyframed overlays,
green screen, sound clean-up and loudness, a Clips window, render
previews, proxies, undo, saving, autosave and export to MP4.

The User Guide
--------------
Press F1 in the editor, or choose User guide from the icon bar menu or
the main menu, to open the full illustrated guide in your web browser.
(It's !ReelEdit.Guide.index/html, if you'd rather open it yourself. If
HTML files open in Edit on your computer, double-click NetSurf once
first.) It explains everything below with examples, for beginners and
for advanced users. This file is the short version.

It needs RISC OS 5 on ARMv7 with VFP (Raspberry Pi 2, 3, 4), with
SharedUnixLibrary and ARMEABISupport (!Run checks). H.264 is decoded on
the Pi's VideoCore (1080p needs gpu_mem=128 in config.txt) and HEVC on the
Pi 4's HEVC block, the ARM taking over for anything they refuse. Sound
plays through SharedSoundBuffer, and the pictures keep time with it.

The editor
----------
Click the icon on the icon bar: the editor opens on a new project, the
Viewer (the picture, the transport and a toolbar) with the Timeline under
it.
Menu over the icon: Info (the version), User guide, Logging (see Log,
below) and Quit.

The Inspector, to the right of the Viewer, shows the settings of what's
selected on the Timeline: a clip's colour (Look, Brightness, Contrast,
Saturation, Warmth, Midtones, Reset) and, on its Sound tab, its sound
(Volume, Mute, Pan, Rumble, Voices, Background, Normalise the level); an
overlay's Place, Size and Opacity (and a title's Edit title...), with a
Sound tab for a video with sound; a sound's Volume, Mute and the same
clean-up. Rumble cuts below 100 Hz; Voices lifts about 3 kHz; Background
turns quiet stretches down; Normalise sets the Volume so the loud parts
come to -3 dB (from the waveform, so it has to be made first). Edit >
Normalise all the sound does every clip and sound in one undo.
A clip's Motion tab: pan and zoom over its length (Zoom in, Zoom out, Pan
left to right, right to left, upwards, downwards; How far: 1.1 to 2
times). A colour clip's Colour tab starts with Fill, its colour.

Wipes: select a dissolve and choose its Transition in the Inspector
(Dissolve, Wipe from the left, from the top, Barn doors, Diagonal,
Circle, Box, Clock), with Softness (its edge) and Direction.

Effects (a clip's Colour tab): Negative, Sketch, Posterise. Green
screen (an overlay's Key out): Green or Blue made see-through, with a
Tolerance. Loudness (the Inspector with nothing selected): the whole
film evened out as it plays and exports, for the web (-14 LUFS) or TV
(-23 LUFS). Edit > Countdown at the playhead: a 5-second leader.

Keyframes (an overlay's Inspector): Keyframes On makes one at its start
and one at the playhead; Place, Size and Opacity then set the keyframe at
the playhead, the overlay moving smoothly between them. Go to the next
keyframe, Delete the keyframe here; Keyframes again: off.

Freeze frames: F (Edit > Freeze the frame) holds the frame at the
playhead for 2 seconds there, the clip split around it. Edit > Colour
clip at the playhead puts in 2 seconds of black (its Fill in the
Inspector). Both are stills: trimmed, faded, coloured as pictures are. Click or drag along a slider (the Viewer shows it as you
drag; a drag is one step for Undo). Ctrl-I (View > Inspector) closes and
opens it.

Adding clips: drag video files from a directory display to the Timeline
(they go in where they're dropped: before the clip there, or after it if
dropped nearer its end), or to the Viewer or the icon bar icon (at the
end). An MLT project (from ReelEdit, Shotcut, Kdenlive or melt) dropped
is opened.

Files that don't say how long they are (raw MPEG-2 video, .m2v, and raw
H.264) are read through first to find their length: "Measuring" and how
far it's got show beside the transport, the desktop going on meanwhile,
and the clip goes in when it's done (files dropped meanwhile follow it).
Escape stops it. An .m2v file holds only the pictures: its sound, if it
has any, is in a file of its own (.mp2, .ac3, .wav), which will go on an
audio track when ReelEdit has them.

Dragging on the Timeline: drag a clip's end (near its right edge, or
just past the last clip's end) to trim it, or its start (near its left
edge; a selected clip shows handles there): the clips after it move along,
and the Viewer shows the frame at the edge as you drag. Drag the middle of
a clip to move it: a line shows where it will go (the nearest cut), and it
goes there when you let go. Each drag is one step for Undo (F8).

Effects (fades and dissolves): press Menu over a clip on the Timeline
for its own menu ("Clip"):
  Fade in, Fade out   a fade from black at its start, or to black at its
                      end, a second long (ticked when it has one: choose
                      it again to take it away); the sound fades too
  Dissolve from the clip before, Dissolve into the clip after
                      a dissolve a second long at that cut (the one shot
                      fading into the other, the sound crossfading), made
                      from the clips' frames beyond the cut if they have
                      any (trimmed clips do), so nothing moves
  Delete, Video information...
Each effect is shown as a box of its own over the part of the clip it
covers: a fade along the top of the clip, a dissolve as a box laid over
the two clips where they overlap (the clips run on under it). Drag a fade's box by its white inner edge,
or a dissolve's ends, to make it longer or shorter; the Viewer shows its
middle when you stop moving. Menu over a dissolve: Delete takes it out
(the clips as they were); or click it and press Delete. Also: D (Edit >
Dissolve at the cut) puts a dissolve at the cut nearest the playhead, and
dragging from the small white mark at the bottom of a cut overlaps the
clips by as far as you drag, the overlap a dissolve. A fade stays at the
clip's end as you trim it; at an end in a dissolve there's none.

Marks (a stretch of the project to play or take out): I marks the in
point at the playhead, O the out point (its frame is the last one in the
stretch); the stretch shows light blue on the ruler, with blue lines
through the track. Shift-I and Shift-O go to the marks; Return plays from
in to out and stops on the out mark. ' (Extract) takes the stretch out,
the clips after it moving up; ; (Lift) takes it out leaving a gap. Both
are one step for Undo; neither goes through a dissolve (delete it first).
Play > Clear the marks takes them away. The marks aren't saved.

Overlays (pictures over the video: a logo, a title made elsewhere, a
frame): drop a PNG (its see-through parts kept), a JPEG or a sprite file
on the Timeline's top row, V2, where you want it to start (or on the
Viewer: at the playhead). It's shown for 5 seconds, or until the next
overlay or the end; a picture as big as the frame (or near it) fills the
frame, a smaller one goes top right as a logo. Drag it along V2 to move
it, its ends to make it shorter or longer; overlays don't overlap. Press
Menu over it for its menu ("Overlay"):
  Place      Full frame, Centre, Top left, Top right, Bottom left,
             Bottom right, Lower third
  Size       the box it's fitted in, 10% to 100% of the frame (it keeps
             its shape)
  Opacity    100%, 75%, 50%, 25%
  Fade in, Fade out   a second each (ticked when it has one)
  Delete
(Adjust keeps the menu open to try another.) A sprite is saved as a PNG
beside its file first ("Logos" sprite "bug" becomes Logos_bug/png; the
sprite file isn't changed), so keep it with the project. A sprite file of
more sprites than one gives a menu to pick from; 32-bit sprites with
alpha, masks and palettes are understood. A picture dropped on V1 is a
clip there, 5 seconds long.

Titles (T, or Edit > New title...): a title goes on a row of its own at
the top of the Timeline (it appears with the first title), so it can be on
screen with anything on V2, and it's always on top. Titles don't overlap
each other: a new one needs room on the titles row at the playhead. Drag
one to move it, its ends to trim it; Menu over it for Edit title..., Place,
Opacity and fades.

Videos on V2 (a picture in the picture, or a cutaway): drop a video on
V2 and it goes over V1 from there, all of it (or up to the next overlay
or the end), bottom right at a third of the size; Place > Full frame
covers V1 completely. Its sound is mixed with V1's. Drag its start or end
to trim it as you would a clip on V1 (its start trims into the video,
not just later on the Timeline); Place, Size, Opacity and the fades work
as for pictures (the fades are of the picture, not the sound). Videos on
V2 are blue, pictures teal. Overlays are saved in the project as MLT's
composite transitions.

The Timeline: the playhead moves only from the ruler (the strip with the
times along the top). Click the ruler to move the playhead there (the
Viewer shows that frame), or drag along it to scrub; its timecode is shown
beside it. Clicks and drags on the tracks never move the playhead: click a
clip or an overlay to select it (Adjust: select or not), click an empty
part to clear the selection. With snapping on (N, or
the Snap button), the playhead snaps to the cuts near it. The scroll
wheel scrolls the Timeline; Ctrl and the wheel zoom it, about the
pointer. The Timeline is attached under the Viewer: move or resize either
and the other follows (View > Attach the Timeline turns that off, and on
again).

The toolbar: Split (at the playhead), Delete (the selected clip or
overlay; the clips after it close up; Adjust: leave a gap), Undo, Redo, Snap; then
"Timeline zoom" - + Fit (these zoom the Timeline, never the picture), and
Save. Interactive help (!Help) says what each does.

The menu (Menu over the Viewer or the Timeline):
  File  New, Save (F3), Save as...
  Edit  Undo, Redo, Split, Trim the start, Trim the end, Dissolve,
        Fade in, Fade out, Delete, Leave gap, Extract, Lift,
        Clear selection
  Play  Play/Stop, Start, End, Mark in, Mark out, Play in to out,
        Clear the marks
  View  Zoom the Timeline in, out, the whole project; Attach the
        Timeline; Snapping; Viewer size; Clips...; Use proxies
  (and File > Export..., Edit > New title..., Cut, Copy, Paste, Roll,
  Play > Render previews, Stop rendering: see the Guide)
  Video information...  the selected clip (or the one at the playhead):
        its file, length, codecs, size, frame rate, sound, and whether
        the VideoCore is decoding it; and the project's size and length

Keys (with the Viewer or the Timeline in front, clicked on):
  Space        play / stop
  J  K  L      play backwards, stop, play forwards; J or L again: faster (x2, x4)
  Left Right   a frame back, forwards (Shift: a second)
  Up Down      the cut before, after
  Home  Copy   the start, the end
  S            split at the playhead
  Q  W         trim the clip at the playhead: its start to it, its end to
               it (the clips after close up)
  I  O         the in mark, the out mark at the playhead
  Shift-I  -O  go to the in mark, the out mark
  Return       play from the in mark to the out mark
  '  ;         Extract (the marked part out, closing up), Lift (a gap)
  D            a dissolve at the cut nearest the playhead
  Delete       delete the selected clip, dissolve or overlay (Shift-Delete:
               leave a gap)
  F8  F9       undo, redo (100 steps)
  N            snapping on / off
  +  -         zoom the Timeline in, out
  Shift-Z  \   the whole project in the Timeline
  Ctrl-Z       clear the selection
  E            roll the cut nearest the playhead to it (Ctrl-drag an edge:
               the same)
  Ctrl-X C V   cut, copy, paste at the playhead
  T            a new title
  Ctrl-B       the Clips window (a source: I, O, then , insert . overwrite)
  Ctrl-R       render previews (the bar under the ruler: red, amber, green)
  Ctrl-E       export
  F3           save
  F1           the User Guide
  Ctrl-I       the Inspector
  Escape       stop

Saving: the standard Save as box: drag the file icon to a directory
display, or type a full name and press Return. Projects are MLT XML
files (filetype XML, &F80), which Shotcut and Kdenlive open too. Closing
the editor with changes not saved asks first.

While playing, the picture goes through the hardware overlay (VideoOverlay)
when it can; a menu or window over the picture, or stopping, draws it in
the window instead. *Set ReelEdit$NoOverlay 1 never uses the overlay.

Log
---
Logging is off as standard. To turn it on, press Menu over the icon on
the icon bar and choose Logging (ticked when it's on); choose it again to
turn it off. ReelEdit remembers the setting (in <Choices$Write>.ReelEdit).
While it's on, ReelEdit writes what it does to <Wimp$ScrapDir>.ReelEditLog,
starting afresh each time it runs. If something goes wrong, turn logging
on, do it again, and send the log with your report.

*Set ReelEdit$Log to a file name to log there from the start (or to "off"
to keep it off), whatever the menu says.

